# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED, AND NOTHING HERE COSTS MONEY.
#
# There is no API key in this file, and there is not meant to be. The brain is
# a model running on your own machine, reached through the bridge; the voice
# runs in your browser; the wake word runs in your browser. Copy this to
# .env.local only if you want to change a default.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* ones
#     from its shell environment.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* ones from .env.local.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser, so nothing sensitive
# belongs behind one.


# ===================================================================
# THE MODEL (the bridge) — the one thing worth setting
# ===================================================================
# JARVIS thinks with a local model. Any OpenAI-compatible server works:
#
#   Ollama       http://localhost:11434/v1   ollama run llama3.1
#   llama.cpp     http://localhost:8080/v1     ./llama-server -m model.gguf
#   LM Studio     http://localhost:1234/v1     (GUI — load a model, start server)
#   vLLM          http://localhost:8000/v1
#
# The defaults below are Ollama's. If your model lives somewhere else, change
# the URL; nothing else needs to move.

# Where the model server listens. Default http://localhost:11434/v1
# JARVIS_MODEL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RAM-aware model pipeline
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no fixed OS/apps/AI split. At bridge startup (and whenever you press
# RESCAN / APPLY in MODEL STACK) the planner samples how much RAM is actually
# free and gives the AI the share you asked for — all of it by default.
#
#   JARVIS_RAM_SHARE=80        # 80% of free RAM; also accepts 0.8 or "80%"
#   JARVIS_RAM_CAP_GB=12       # optional hard ceiling, wins when it is smaller
#
# Whatever you do not allocate simply stays free for everything else on the
# machine. The MODEL STACK panel writes models/ram-allocation.json so a choice
# made in the HUD survives a restart; JARVIS_RAM_CONFIG relocates that file.
# Leave both unset and the bridge uses 100% of free RAM with no hard cap.
#
# Free RAM means MemAvailable on Linux (reclaimable cache counts as free) and
# os.freemem() elsewhere. Chat, vision and coding share one Qwen3.5 multimodal
# tag when the allocation fits it; a larger allocation reaches higher-parameter
# rungs, a tighter one steps down honestly. Repeated tags count once and can
# stay warm across shared routes; switching to a different local tag or Whisper
# unloads the retained model first. The plan is an estimate, not a guarantee.
# When the allocation is too small for even the 0.873B Q8_0 model it is shown
# as best-effort only; it is not pulled or invoked.
#
# Chat, vision and coding use abliterated instruct builds. Vision is a required
# slot and every reference tier advertises its multimodal model honestly. The
# native 122B tag (125B parameters, Q4_K_M, ~81 GB download / 96 GB estimated
# resident) is only selected when your allocation is at least that large.
# Vision capability is confirmed before pixels are sent.
# Whisper uses multilingual GGML files.
# The root build.ps1/build.sh scripts check/install only the detected fitting
# tier. They do not pull every catalogue rung; npm run setup remains read-only.
#
# `chat` handles multilingual chat and translates intent into English.
# `vision` describes images. `reason` receives English coding prompts.
# `speech` is local multilingual Whisper STT. Browser/system or Kokoro TTS is
# selected by RAM autopilot separately below.

# Optional manual overrides. Leave unset for RAM autopilot. These explicit
# overrides can bypass the RAM planner, so check the model's resident needs.
# JARVIS_MODEL_CHAT=huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:9b-q8_0
# JARVIS_MODEL_VISION=huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:9b-q8_0
# JARVIS_MODEL_REASON=huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:9b-q8_0
# Pin every route to one model instead of the RAM-planned shared rung.
# JARVIS_MODEL_NAME=huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:9b-q8_0

# Per-slot server URLs; each defaults to JARVIS_MODEL_BASE_URL.
# JARVIS_MODEL_CHAT_URL=
# JARVIS_MODEL_VISION_URL=
# JARVIS_MODEL_REASON_URL=

# Ollama management API root for pull/status/unload operations.
# JARVIS_OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The model runtime the setup page can download for itself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one-click install does not run an installer. It resolves the right
# standalone Ollama archive for this machine from the release's own asset list,
# downloads it (resuming if the connection drops), verifies the published
# SHA-256, unpacks it inside this project with Node itself, and starts it. All
# of that is a folder you can delete, and nothing is added to PATH.
#
#   JARVIS_RUNTIME_DIR=models/runtime      where it is unpacked
#   JARVIS_RUNTIME_MODELS=models/ollama    where that runtime keeps model weights
#   JARVIS_RUNTIME_BASE=<url>              mirror or pinned release download root
#   JARVIS_RUNTIME_API=<url>               release metadata (default: GitHub
#                                          latest release for ollama/ollama)
#
# A model server already answering on the model port is always preferred, and a
# system `ollama` on PATH is used when this project has not downloaded one.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instruct, never base — and abliterated is not base
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A BASE model predicts the next token. It has never been taught that a function
# call is a thing it can emit, so it will DESCRIBE calling a tool instead of
# calling one, and JARVIS will appear to ignore everything he can do. No error,
# no crash — just silence where an action should have been.
#
# "Uncensored" and "base" get conflated constantly. They are not the same thing.
# Base means unaligned AND unteachable: it will not refuse you, and it will not
# understand you either. It is a starting point for fine-tuning, not a chat
# model.
#
# The defaults above are ABLITERATED INSTRUCT builds. Ablation removes the
# internal direction that produces refusals, so the model answers without
# hedging — but because the ablation is applied to an instruct build, it still
# follows instructions and still calls tools. That is what "uncensored" should
# mean here, and it is what you have been given.
#
# Ollama's Qwen3.5 abliterated tags are instruct builds. Examples:
#
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:0.8b
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:4B-q8_0
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:9b-q8_0
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:27b
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen3.5-abliterated:122B  # ~81 GB, workstation-only
#
# The 32 GB profile adds this higher-parameter HF text/coding candidate:
#
#   ollama pull hf.co/mradermacher/Huihui-Qwen3.5-27B-abliterated-GGUF:Q2_K
#
# Its Ollama HF import has not been exercised here. Vision remains routed to
# the native 9.65B Q8_0 tag and is capability-checked before image input.
# `-base` variants are not suitable tool-calling substitutes; use an
# abliterated instruct build as above.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sampling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Some servers insist on a non-empty Authorization header even with no auth.
# Local ones do not. Leave this alone unless yours complains.
# JARVIS_MODEL_API_KEY=

# Sampling temperature. Low on purpose — a spoken answer should not be
# adventurous. Default 0.6.
# JARVIS_MODEL_TEMPERATURE=0.6

# How many tool-calling rounds one question may take. A local model loops more
# than a hosted one does — it re-reads the same page, asks for the same
# screenshot twice — so this ceiling matters. Default 8.
# JARVIS_MODEL_MAX_TURNS=8

# Whole-turn timeout in ms, tools and all. Local models are slow; do not cut
# them off. Default 180000 (three minutes).
# JARVIS_MODEL_TIMEOUT_MS=180000


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — the rest of the JARVIS_* variables
# ===================================================================

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` sets
# this to 1 for you — read the decideTool gate in bridge/server.mjs first.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# The command line (jarvis_shell). `allowlist` (the default) refuses any program
# that is not on the list, and names the program in the refusal; `full` lets any
# program run. The deny list -- formatting disks, recursive deletes of / ~ or *,
# piping a download into a shell, power changes -- holds in both modes.
# JARVIS_SHELL_MODE=allowlist

# Extra programs for the allowlist, comma separated. Names are enough: add
# `mytool`, not `/usr/local/bin/mytool`. A bare `*` lifts the program check
# without switching modes.
# JARVIS_SHELL_ALLOW=

# Extra working-directory roots for run_command, comma separated. Home, the temp
# directory and the project are always allowed.
# JARVIS_SHELL_ROOTS=

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# The terminal client (npm run cli) connects to the bridge on
# ws://localhost:JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT unless it is told otherwise.
# JARVIS_CLI_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Optional whisper.cpp binary/model overrides. The Model Stack installer saves
# its selected multilingual model under models/ and installs a local runtime.
# JARVIS_WHISPER_BIN=whisper-cli
# JARVIS_WHISPER_MODEL=models/ggml-base-q5_1.bin

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# Extra directories the bridge's /file endpoint may serve from, comma separated.
# Your home directory and the system temp directory are always included; this
# is for anything outside them, such as an external drive of footage.
# Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Speech-to-text input engine. Default auto follows the RAM plan: local
# multilingual Whisper when selected/installed, otherwise browser recognition
# (which may use the browser vendor's service). Set whisper to force local-only.
# VITE_STT_ENGINE=auto

# Browser text-to-speech engine. Default auto follows the RAM plan.
#   system — built-in browser/OS SpeechSynthesis; instant and no model download.
#   kokoro — browser-local neural speech, cached on first use when selected.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=auto

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# All four are British male: bm_george, bm_fable, bm_lewis, bm_daniel.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george
